WEBVTT

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:30.250
<v Ira Glass>OK, so before the president got coronavirus, which is sending everything now into some new and unforeseen reality, there were certain things about the election that had settled into predictable rhythms, right? The president will talk about rioters in the streets and law and order. Joe Biden will talk about Donald Trump's character and the way he's done his job.
<v Ira Glass>好的，在总统感染新冠病毒——这让一切都陷入了某种未知的现实——之前，选举中有一些事情已经形成了可预测的节奏，对吧？总统会谈论街头的暴徒以及法律与秩序。乔·拜登会谈论唐纳德·特朗普的品格以及他履行职责的方式。

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:46.970
<v Ira Glass>And when it came to the actual process of voting and the mail-in ballots that are going to be used by record numbers of people this year, the talking points lined up like this. The president said that mail-in ballots were going to be a disaster. Joe Biden said they were a perfectly good way to vote. The experts said the evidence backed up Biden.
<v Ira Glass>而在实际的投票过程以及今年将有创纪录人数使用的邮寄选票方面，谈话要点是这样的：总统说邮寄选票将是一场灾难。乔·拜登说它们是完美的投票方式。专家们说证据支持拜登。

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:59.120
<v Ira Glass>Then the president would again insist that they are a disaster. Biden would again say no. The experts would again back him up. The president again would call them a disaster, around and around and around in a circle that never ended, a very tedious circle.
<v Ira Glass>然后总统会再次坚称它们是一场灾难。拜登会再次说不。专家们会再次支持他。总统再次称它们为灾难，就这样周而复始，陷入了一个永无止境、非常乏味的循环。

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:05.370
<v Ira Glass>But, you know, we can remove ourselves from that circle, right? For a little perspective.
<v Ira Glass>但是，你知道，我们可以跳出那个圈子，对吧？为了获得一点视角。

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.270
<v Ira Glass>[DIAL TONE]
<v Ira Glass>[拨号音]

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:09.980
<v Wendy Smith>Good evening, town of Nokomis.
<v Wendy Smith>晚上好，诺科米斯镇。

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:21.120
<v Ira Glass>For a few weeks now, one of our producers, Ben Calhoun, has been calling around to the people who actually handle these ballots that everybody has been yelling about. These people touch them with their hands, and they stack them in stacks. Ben?
<v Ira Glass>几周以来，我们的制片人之一本·卡尔霍恩一直在给那些真正处理这些每个人都在大声疾呼的选票的人打电话。这些人亲手接触这些选票，并将它们一叠一叠地码放好。本？

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:34.850
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, so the person you're hearing, this is Wendy Smith. She's the person who's responsible for running the election and dealing with mail-in ballots and all that in a small town in my home state, which is Wisconsin. She's in Nokomis, which is way up north.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，你刚才听到的是温迪·史密斯。她负责在我家乡威斯康星州的一个小镇上组织选举、处理邮寄选票等事宜。她在诺科米斯，那是很靠北的地方。

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:49.880
<v Ben Calhoun>And I called Wendy because I was wondering how this bizarre year was going for clerks like her. I had seen Wendy in a newspaper story. She had tattoos on both arms and this T-shirt that said, "Why should only one hour be happy?" And it said that she rode motorcycles.
<v Ben Calhoun>我打电话给温迪，因为我想知道这个怪异的一年对像她这样的职员来说是怎么过的。我曾在报纸上看到过关于温迪的报道。她双臂上有纹身，穿着一件写着“为什么只有一小时是快乐的？”的T恤，上面还说她骑摩托车。

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:58.010
<v Ben Calhoun>And I just kind of thought I wanted to talk to her. When I originally reached out to her, she was busy sending out absentee ballots to people who'd requested them.
<v Ben Calhoun>我只是觉得我想和她聊聊。我最初联系她时，她正忙着给那些申请了缺席选票的人寄出选票。

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:02.850
<v Wendy Smith>You don't mind if I work and talk at the same time, do you?
<v Wendy Smith>你不介意我边工作边说话吧？

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:06.530
<v Ben Calhoun>No, no, I actually imagine that you have a lot to do right now.
<v Ben Calhoun>不，不，我其实能想象你现在有很多工作要做。

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:12.030
<v Wendy Smith>Yeah, I came in to 29 absentee ballots, and I've got a stack more that have to go.
<v Wendy Smith>是的，我收到了29张缺席选票，还有一堆需要处理。

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:17.310
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, wait, so how many total are you looking at compared to a normal year?
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，等等，那么与正常年份相比，你总共处理了多少？

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:22.570
<v Wendy Smith>Oh my gosh. My last presidential, I had 100 absentee voters--
<v Wendy Smith>天哪。我上次总统大选时，有100名缺席选民——

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:22.920
<v Ben Calhoun>Total.
<v Ben Calhoun>总共。

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:30.800
<v Wendy Smith>--four years ago-- total. I've already almost tripled for this election what I did four years ago.
<v Wendy Smith>——四年前——总共。这次选举我已经处理了差不多是四年前三倍的数量。

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.270
<v Ben Calhoun>Tripled. So that's like 300 voters out of maybe 1,000 that she's responsible for.
<v Ben Calhoun>三倍。所以这大概是她负责的1000名选民中的300人左右。

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:36.840
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:48.650
<v Ben Calhoun>And she's getting more requests every week. I've talked to Wendy a couple times now, actually. And both times, she has worked the entire time that we've been talking. You can hear her keyboard and her printer going in the background the whole time.
<v Ben Calhoun>而且她每周收到的申请越来越多。其实我已经和温迪聊过几次了。在这期间，我们谈话时她一直都在工作。你可以听到她键盘和打印机在后台工作的声音。

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:57.630
<v Wendy Smith>As I've been talking to you, I'm printing off all my absentee ballot requests because I have to have them open and done and in the mail today.
<v Wendy Smith>刚才和你谈话的时候，我正在打印我所有的缺席选票申请，因为我必须把它们全部处理完，并在今天寄出。

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:09.780
<v Ben Calhoun>Wendy told me in all her years as clerk, this is definitely the most work she's ever had to do for elections. And one of the reasons for that is that the legislature and the governor keep fighting over how everything should run.
<v Ben Calhoun>温迪告诉我，在她担任职员的所有年份里，这绝对是她为了选举必须做的最繁重的工作。原因之一是立法机构和州长一直在争论所有事情应该如何运作。

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:20.760
<v Ben Calhoun>There are also lawsuits in Wisconsin, like there are all over the country, about what the rules for the election should be. And all the little changes that come out of that stuff can be a ton of extra work for Wendy.
<v Ben Calhoun>和全国各地一样，威斯康星州也有关于选举规则的诉讼。由此产生的所有细小变化都可能给温迪带来大量额外的工作。

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:39.240
<v Ben Calhoun>Like, in Wisconsin, at one point, they changed the rule about how many days somebody has to be a resident in order to vote. So Wendy started scrambling to fix all that. She's redoing all these envelopes. And then they just changed the rule back, meaning that all of that scrambling and work was for nothing.
<v Ben Calhoun>比如，在威斯康星州，他们曾一度改变了投票所需居住天数的规定。所以温迪开始手忙脚乱地修正这一切。她重做了所有这些信封。然后他们又把规定改了回去，这意味着之前所有的忙乱和工作都白费了。

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:49.880
<v Wendy Smith>So then you went back to trusting your envelopes. Ugh. So all the envelopes that I pre-- you're laughing, but try being a one-man-show doing this.
<v Wendy Smith>所以你又回去相信你的那些信封了。呃。所以我所有的预先……你在笑，但试着成为一个独自处理这一切的人吧。

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:58.520
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS] No, I'm just so sorry. I imagine you-- I just had an image of you buying Pepto Bismol at Costco by the case or something.
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑] 不，我很抱歉。我能想象你……我脑海里浮现出你在Costco整箱购买胃药或者类似东西的画面。

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.330
<v Wendy Smith>Yeah.
<v Wendy Smith>是的。

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:11.730
<v Ben Calhoun>And compared to a normal year, how many more times work do you feel like you're doing this year on elections?
<v Ben Calhoun>与正常年份相比，你觉得今年在选举上投入的工作量多了多少倍？

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.170
<v Wendy Smith>It's a full-time job.
<v Wendy Smith>这是一份全职工作。

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.970
<v Ben Calhoun>Is this your only job?
<v Ben Calhoun>这是你唯一的工作吗？

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.910
<v Wendy Smith>No, this is my part-time job.
<v Wendy Smith>不，这是我的兼职工作。

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:23.100
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS] What do you do when you're not doing this?
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑] 你不做这个的时候做什么？

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:30.240
<v Wendy Smith>I work for an insurance company. I'm still working plus putting over time in there plus working here.
<v Wendy Smith>我在一家保险公司工作。我还在工作，加上在那边加班，再加上在这里工作。

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.230
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh my gosh.
<v Ben Calhoun>天哪。

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.100
<v Wendy Smith>Which is fine.
<v Wendy Smith>没关系的。

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.360
<v Ben Calhoun>When do you sleep?
<v Ben Calhoun>你什么时候睡觉？

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.820
<v Wendy Smith>Um--
<v Wendy Smith>嗯——

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:42.150
<v Ben Calhoun>[LAUGHS]
<v Ben Calhoun>[笑]

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.060
<v Ira Glass>OK, so she's got the rule changes. She's got tons of extra ballots she's got to keep track of.
<v Ira Glass>好的，所以她要处理规则变更，还要追踪大量额外的选票。

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:59.660
<v Ben Calhoun>Mm-hmm, and the other big thing that's taking up a bunch more time is helping voters. Because people will just fill things out wrong, or they don't know where their ballot is. I talked to her the other day, and she was telling me about this couple.
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯，另一件占用大量时间的事情是帮助选民。因为人们总是把事情填错，或者不知道他们的选票在哪里。前几天我和她聊天，她跟我说起一对夫妇。

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:13.730
<v Wendy Smith>I got to try to locate their number. They're snowbirds. Their ballot went down to Florida. They're not there yet. The ballot came back, and now I got to try to find a way to reach them.
<v Wendy Smith>我得设法找到他们的号码。他们是候鸟型居民。他们的选票被寄到了佛罗里达。他们还没到那儿。选票被退回来了，现在我得设法找到联系他们的方法。

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.230
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, wow.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，哇。

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:22.320
<v Wendy Smith>Right, so I get to dig through about 300 applications to try to find their-- see if they have a phone number.
<v Wendy Smith>对，所以我得翻遍大约300份申请，试图找到他们的……看看他们是否有电话号码。

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:30.410
<v Ben Calhoun>So Wendy was trying to find them, which it seems like something she kind of does a lot. She digs up numbers for people. She finds people on Facebook.
<v Ben Calhoun>所以温迪在努力找到他们，这似乎是她常做的事。她为人们找号码，她在Facebook上找人。

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:38.730
<v Wendy Smith>So now, I mean, I know where they live. I can probably drive over there when I'm done with work and say, hey, what do you want to do?
<v Wendy Smith>所以现在，我的意思是，我知道他们住哪儿。下班后我可能会开车过去，说，嘿，你们想怎么办？

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:45.660
<v Ben Calhoun>The clerks that I've been talking to, they all kind of talk like this. They have this down-to-earth, sort of civic-mindedness.
<v Ben Calhoun>我采访过的那些职员，说话风格都很像这样。他们有一种脚踏实地的、某种公民意识。

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:46.730
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:05:46.730 --> 00:06:05.750
<v Ben Calhoun>There's this other clerk, Marilynn Pedretti. She's in this town called Holland, which is slightly bigger than Nokomis. It's like 4,300 people. And Marilynn Pedretti spent five years in DC, where she worked for a congressman and then she worked in the Senate. But she got sick of Washington, and so she spent the last 20 years as the town clerk for Holland.
<v Ben Calhoun>还有另一位职员，玛丽琳·佩德雷蒂。她在荷兰镇，比诺科米斯稍微大一点。大约有4300人。玛丽琳·佩德雷蒂在华盛顿特区工作了五年，她曾为一位国会议员工作，然后在参议院工作。但她厌倦了华盛顿，所以过去20年她一直担任荷兰镇的镇职员。

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:27.710
<v Marilynn Pedretti>I want votes to count. I will not disenfranchise somebody and be lazy. I will do everything I can. I try to find their phone number first. If I can't find that, I try to email. If I don't have that, I'll send them a letter. So yeah, we're doing everything we can to make sure those votes count.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我希望选票能被计入。我不会剥夺某人的权利并变得懒惰。我会尽我所能。我先试着找他们的电话号码。如果找不到，我就试着发电子邮件。如果还是没有，我就给他们寄一封信。所以是的，我们正在竭尽全力确保这些选票被计入。

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:45.300
<v Marilynn Pedretti>The one thing I find very frustrating is the conspiracy theories that are out there. And people believe them. I'm like, hello, I'm your town clerk. I am not going to throw your ballot away. And you can track it. There's no way I can throw your ballot away.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我感到非常沮丧的一点是外界存在的阴谋论。而且人们相信这些。我就像在说，你好，我是你的镇职员。我不会把你的选票扔掉。而且你可以追踪它。我根本没法扔掉你的选票。

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:57.920
<v Marilynn Pedretti>So when you hear these stories about somebody picked some ballots out of a garbage and they open them up, it's like number one, whoever opened them up should be put in jail because you cannot open up the ballot. If it's sealed, you have to leave it sealed until election day.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>所以当你听到有人从垃圾桶里挑出一些选票并打开它们的故事时，第一，任何打开它们的人都应该被关进监狱，因为你不能打开选票。如果它是密封的，你必须保持密封直到选举日。

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:21.700
<v Marilynn Pedretti>And we have 1,800 municipalities in the state of Wisconsin. It's not centralized. It would take 1,800 clerks to do the wrong thing. Are there clerks out there that make mistakes? You bet. But when people come in with these conspiracies that the whole system is rigged, I'm like, no. No, it's not.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>威斯康星州有1800个市镇。这不是中心化的。这需要1800名职员都做错事才行。有没有职员犯错误？当然有。但当人们带着这种“整个系统都被操纵了”的阴谋论进来时，我就说，不，不，根本不是这样。

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:42.930
<v Ben Calhoun>All the clerks that I talked to-- and this is in Republican areas and Democratic areas. They all seemed like this was sort of driving them a little nuts, people getting the idea that what they do is very loosely goosey and that people are just going to cheat the system. To them, that's just outlandish because they say that there's too much security built into everything.
<v Ben Calhoun>我采访的所有职员——包括共和党和民主党地区——都觉得这让他们有点抓狂，人们总觉得他们的工作非常随意，觉得人们会欺骗系统。对他们来说，这简直是荒谬的，因为他们说系统里嵌入了太多的安全性。

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.620
<v Ira Glass>Security like what?
<v Ira Glass>什么样的安全性？

00:07:44.620 --> 00:08:06.250
<v Ben Calhoun>Well, they keep track of every request for a ballot. Unless you can't leave the house, you have to submit your ID to get an absentee ballot. Clerks put tracking info on the ballot. And then they keep track of them when they're sent out and then coming back. And then when it comes back in, it has to have a voter signature and a witness signature for verification.
<v Ben Calhoun>嗯，他们追踪每一份选票申请。除非你无法离开家，否则你必须提交身份证件才能获得缺席选票。职员会在选票上贴上追踪信息。然后他们会在选票寄出和收回时进行追踪。当它被收回时，必须有选民签名和证人签名以供核实。

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:16.790
<v Ben Calhoun>At the end of the whole election, they also have to have report back to the state what ballots ended up where, like which ones went out and came back and which ones went out and never came back.
<v Ben Calhoun>在整个选举结束时，他们还必须向州政府报告哪些选票流向了哪里，比如哪些发出了并收回了，哪些发出后就没回来。

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:27.710
<v Ben Calhoun>Say somebody moves and they ask for a ballot in a new location, that's also something the state tracks. And I just want to say, Wisconsin is pretty typical for how all this stuff works.
<v Ben Calhoun>假设某人搬家了，他们在新的地方申请选票，这也是州政府追踪的事项之一。我想说，威斯康星州在处理这些事情的流程上非常典型。

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:45.850
<v Ira Glass>I feel like all these details exist in this universe that doesn't overlap in any way with the discussion that I see in the national media and the arguments that I heard during the presidential debate. It just seems kind of sad that that universe, the national politics universe, is lobbing grenades into these women's universe.
<v Ira Glass>我觉得所有这些细节都存在于这个宇宙中，与我在全国性媒体讨论中看到的、以及在总统辩论中听到的争论毫无重叠。真令人难过，那个全国政治的宇宙竟然向这些女性的宇宙投掷手榴弹。

00:08:45.850 --> 00:09:01.790
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, I know. I mean, I've been at this for a couple weeks, and I haven't found one clerk that is worried about fraud. All of them are worried about the rule changes and the number of extra mail-in ballots. I mean, they think they can deal with that. It's just a lot more work.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，我知道。我的意思是，我做这个已经几周了，还没发现一个担心欺诈的职员。他们担心的都是规则变化和额外邮寄选票的数量。他们认为他们能应付这些。只是工作量大多了。

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:05.050
<v Ira Glass>But they're running into these conspiracy theories a lot-- locally.
<v Ira Glass>但他们在本地频繁遇到这些阴谋论。

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:17.160
<v Ben Calhoun>Yeah, I know. Yeah, yeah, no, that's what I'm hearing. Marilynn, she's like Wendy. She's a clerk part-time. And she also gardens two acres, and she sells vegetables on the side of the highway.
<v Ben Calhoun>是的，我知道。是的，是的，不，我听到的就是这样。玛丽琳，她就像温迪。她是兼职职员。她还种了两英亩的蔬菜，在公路边卖蔬菜。

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:28.520
<v Marilynn Pedretti>I mean, I have a roadside stand, and I'm out there. And people know who I am. And they'll talk to me about election stuff, which is fun. But this woman who's out there buying my vegetables and knows that I'm the town clerk is telling me that all these dead people are voting.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我的意思是，我在路边有个摊位，我常在那里。人们知道我是谁。他们会跟我谈论选举的事情，这很有趣。但那个买我蔬菜并知道我是镇职员的女士，却在跟我说，所有死人都参加了投票。

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:45.640
<v Marilynn Pedretti>And it's like, um, no. It's pretty much impossible in the state of Wisconsin for a dead person to vote. If they had died the day before election, maybe I'll not catch that, and maybe that ballot will go through. But that's one vote. It's not like the whole cemetery is voting.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我当时就像，嗯，不。在威斯康星州，死人投票几乎是不可能的。如果他们选举前一天去世，也许我没能及时发现，也许那张选票会通过。但这只是一票。并不是整个墓地的人都在投票。

00:09:45.640 --> 00:10:04.380
<v Marilynn Pedretti>And I just looked at her, said, ahem, you know I'm the clerk, right? Yeah, yeah. Do you think I would let dead people vote? Well, no, not you, but somewhere else. I said, can't happen in the state of Wisconsin. Well, um, it happens in other states. No, it doesn't. Stop listening to that stuff.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我看着她，说，咳咳，你知道我是职员，对吧？是的，是的。你认为我会让死人投票吗？好吧，不，不是你，是在别的地方。我说，在威斯康星州不可能发生。好吧，嗯，在别的州会发生。不，不会。别再听那些东西了。

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:13.840
<v Marilynn Pedretti>And I do it cheerfully. I don't yell or anything. Just like, this is-- it's just wrong, and you've got to stop listening to that stuff.
<v Marilynn Pedretti>我非常愉快地做这件事。我不大喊大叫。只是说，这是——这是不对的，你必须停止听那些东西。

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:15.670
<v Ira Glass>Well, Ben Calhoun, thanks very much.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，本·卡尔霍恩，非常感谢。

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:16.710
<v Ben Calhoun>Oh, sure.
<v Ben Calhoun>噢，不客气。

00:10:16.710 --> 00:10:20.440
<v Ira Glass>Are you going to stay in touch with these clerks as everything heats up in the next couple weeks?
<v Ira Glass>随着未来几周形势愈演愈烈，你会与这些职员保持联系吗？

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:26.860
<v Ben Calhoun>I mean, as long as they'll answer my calls.
<v Ben Calhoun>只要他们愿意接听我的电话。

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:37.270
<v Ira Glass>There are so many jobs like this around the country. Some of them are part-time, like with these two women. Some of them are full-time. Some are paid jobs, lots of volunteer or people get some tiny stipend.
<v Ira Glass>全国各地有这么多这样的工作。其中一些是兼职的，就像这两位女性一样。一些是全职的。有些是有报酬的，很多是志愿的或者只领一点微薄的津贴。

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:49.600
<v Ira Glass>These are people who are throwing themselves into the details of some tasks that just needs to get done for our country to function and doing it in the normal, decent, civic-minded way that you would imagine and that you would hope for.
<v Ira Glass>这些人全身心地投入到一些为了国家正常运转而必须完成的琐事中，并以你所能想象和希望的那种正常、体面、有公民意识的方式来处理它们。

00:10:49.600 --> 00:11:10.710
<v Ira Glass>And it's corny to say it, but we are a country blessed with armies of people who are trying to contribute to their communities in all kinds of ways. And today's program is about a group of people like that, people who are, as best we can tell, trying to do a good job for all of us. They're trying to protect the public. But in this case, too often, they're failing.
<v Ira Glass>说这话可能有点陈词滥调，但我们国家确实幸运地拥有成群结队的人，他们正以各种方式为社区做出贡献。今天的节目就是关于这样一群人，据我们所知，他们正在努力为我们所有人做好工作。他们试图保护公众。但在这种情况下，他们往往失败了。

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:25.940
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:46.180
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Clinical Trial. So the people that most of today's program is about are doctors, doctors who've agreed to sit on panels to judge other doctors, to discipline them or revoke their licenses when they behave badly. Every state has these medical boards. They grant medical licenses, and they're the ones responsible for taking them away.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，临床试验。今天节目的大部分内容是关于医生的，那些同意组成小组来评判其他医生、在他们表现恶劣时进行纪律处分或吊销执照的医生。每个州都有这些医疗委员会。他们授予医疗执照，也是负责收回执照的人。

00:11:46.180 --> 00:12:04.540
<v Ira Glass>We heard about all this from a law professor at Vanderbilt, Rebecca Allensworth. In 2018, she started going to medical board hearings in Tennessee, where she lives. It was research for a book that she plans to write about licensing boards. And what she saw when she got there horrified her. Dana Chivvis is going to take it from here.
<v Ira Glass>我们是从范德比尔特大学的法律教授丽贝卡·艾伦斯沃思那里听说这一切的。2018年，她开始参加她居住的田纳西州的医疗委员会听证会。这是她计划撰写的一本关于许可委员会的书的研究内容。当她到达那里时，她所看到的令她感到震惊。达纳·奇维斯将接手下面的内容。

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:22.720
<v Dana Chivvis>When it comes to the Tennessee Board of Medical Examiners, Rebecca is like the one die-hard fan in the stands at a town softball game. She knows all the board members by name. She's interviewed most of them, attended all their meetings in person, or watched the livestream. She even went to their offsite retreat last year.
<v Dana Chivvis>谈到田纳西州医疗审查委员会时，丽贝卡就像小镇垒球比赛看台上唯一的铁杆粉丝。她能叫出所有委员会成员的名字。她采访了他们中的大多数人，亲自参加了所有的会议，或者观看了直播。去年她甚至参加了他们的外部研讨会。

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:27.590
<v Dana Chivvis>Before you went to any medical board meetings, how did you imagine they would be?
<v Dana Chivvis>在你去参加任何医疗委员会会议之前，你是怎么想象它们的？

00:12:27.590 --> 00:12:41.960
<v Rebecca Allensworth>I thought they'd be pretty boring. But I think I also thought it was going to be in some sort of grand state government building. I think I had our state capital in mind or something. But it's just like an office park.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>我以为它们会很无聊。但我认为我也以为它会在某种宏伟的州政府大楼里举行。我想我当时想的是州议会大厦什么的。但它就像一个办公园区。

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.480
<v Dana Chivvis>So yeah, so describe what it actually looked like.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以，描述一下它实际上看起来是什么样的。

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:58.100
<v Rebecca Allensworth>It's like a three or four-story square office building probably built in the '70s or '80s. This is Nashville, so there's copious parking and--
<v Rebecca Allensworth>就像一栋可能建于70年代或80年代的三四层高的方形办公楼。这是纳什维尔，所以停车位很充足，还有——

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.530
<v Dana Chivvis>It sounds like Dunder Mifflin to me.
<v Dana Chivvis>听起来像《办公室》里的邓德·米夫林公司。

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:05.390
<v Rebecca Allensworth>Oh my god, it looked just like Dunder Mifflin. It looked just like Dunder Mifflin.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>天哪，它看起来简直就像邓德·米夫林。看起来一模一样。

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:27.560
<v Dana Chivvis>The first meeting she went to, an OB-GYN appeared before the board to request a new medical license. The board had taken his old license away six months before when they learned that he'd had sex with 11 of his patients. He'd done drugs with at least one of them, too. He'd also prescribe them and other people who were not his patients controlled substances, like codeine and Xanax.
<v Dana Chivvis>她参加的第一次会议上，一位妇产科医生出现在委员会面前，请求获得新的医疗执照。六个月前，当委员会得知他曾与11名患者发生性关系时，撤销了他的旧执照。他也至少与其中一名患者一起吸过毒。他还向她们以及其他非他患者的人开具受控物质，如可待因和赞安诺。

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:47.960
<v Dana Chivvis>So this doctor was there to ask the medical board for a new license. All that stuff, the sex, the drugs, had happened years before. He'd been to rehab, was sober now, saw the error in his old ways. And the board gave him a new license with some conditions attached, but he could practice medicine again. Rebecca couldn't believe it.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以这位医生到那里是请求医疗委员会给他一个新的执照。所有这些事，性、毒品，都发生在几年前。他已经去过康复中心，现在清醒了，看到了他旧日的错误。委员会给了他一个新的执照，并附加了一些条件，但他可以再次从医。丽贝卡简直不敢相信。

00:13:47.960 --> 00:14:03.860
<v Rebecca Allensworth>There just has to be some sins that are unforgivable in the eyes of the board. The board is a regulatory body. They're not your priest. They're not deciding whether or not you're really sorry. They're deciding whether or not you're a safe provider.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>在委员会眼中，一定有一些罪行是不可饶恕的。委员会是一个监管机构。他们不是你的牧师。他们不是在决定你是否真的感到抱歉。他们是在决定你是否是一个安全的医务提供者。

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:14.770
<v Dana Chivvis>Officially, the board's mission is to protect the health, safety, and welfare of people in the state of Tennessee. Board members and staff repeat that mission all the time, like a mantra.
<v Dana Chivvis>正式地，委员会的使命是保护田纳西州人民的健康、安全和福祉。委员会成员和工作人员一直重复这一使命，就像咒语一样。

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:31.110
<v Dana Chivvis>But since Rebecca has been following the board, she's seen a bunch of cases like that OB-GYN, where doctors have done something really bad, seemingly irredeemable, and somehow, they've managed to keep their medical licenses. They're out there, working as doctors in Tennessee right now.
<v Dana Chivvis>但自从丽贝卡开始关注委员会以来，她看到了很多像那位妇产科医生一样的案例，医生们做了一些非常糟糕、看似不可救药的事情，但不知何故，他们设法保住了医疗执照。他们现在就在田纳西州作为医生工作。

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:46.180
<v Dana Chivvis>So this became the question of her research. What was going wrong in the system? Why were these doctors allowed to keep working? One case in particular became the focus of her attention because it seemed like a perfect storm of regulatory failure.
<v Dana Chivvis>于是这成了她研究的问题。系统中出了什么问题？为什么这些医生被允许继续工作？其中一个案例特别引起了她的注意，因为它看起来像是监管失败的完美风暴。

00:14:46.180 --> 00:15:01.870
<v Dana Chivvis>And that's what I want to tell you about today. It's about a doctor named Michael LaPaglia. I'll start the story back in 2013. LaPaglia was working as an ER doctor at the time. And late one night, police got a call about a domestic disturbance at his house.
<v Dana Chivvis>这就是我今天要告诉你的。关于一位名叫迈克尔·拉帕利亚的医生。我把故事带回到2013年。拉帕利亚当时是一名急诊室医生。一天深夜，警察接到一个关于他家里家庭纠纷的电话。

00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:09.190
<v Dana Chivvis>When the police arrived, they smelled weed and searched the house. They found-- actually, let me just read from the police officer's affidavit.
<v Dana Chivvis>警察到达时，闻到了大麻的味道并搜查了房子。他们发现了——事实上，让我读一下警察的宣誓书。

00:15:09.190 --> 00:15:35.080
<v Dana Chivvis>Quote, "A search of the residence pursuant to the search warrant revealed 45 quart-sized Mason jars which contained marijuana, 127 glass pipes used to smoke marijuana, Glock 19 9 millimeter, grow light with ballast, timer, digital scale, and ledger detailing names/amounts. Also discovered were approximately 52 diazepam, 22 amphetamine, and 24 oxycodone pills."
<v Dana Chivvis>引用：“根据搜查令对住宅进行的搜查发现了45个装有大麻的夸脱大小的梅森罐，127个用于吸食大麻的玻璃烟斗，一把格洛克19 9毫米手枪，带有镇流器的生长灯，定时器，数字秤，以及详细记录了姓名/金额的分类账。此外还发现了大约52片地西泮，22片安非他命和24片羟考酮药丸。”

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:50.200
<v Dana Chivvis>In addition, the police found on a shelf in LaPaglia's office vials of fentanyl, morphine, and propofol, which is used for general anesthesia. LaPaglia's girlfriend told police that he had threatened to use that stuff to kill her.
<v Dana Chivvis>此外，警察在拉帕利亚办公室的架子上发现了芬太尼、吗啡和异丙酚，后者用于全身麻醉。拉帕利亚的女友告诉警察，他曾威胁要用这些东西杀死她。

00:15:50.200 --> 00:16:13.990
<v Dana Chivvis>In the petition she filed for a restraining order the next day, she said, quote, "Michael told me that if I ever contacted the police for help or reported his drug use, Michael would use his powers as a physician to have me committed to psychiatric facility. Michael stated he would make sure my body wouldn't be found. Michael stated that he would take my life away. I'm in fear for my life and my son's life."
<v Dana Chivvis>在第二天她提交的限制令申请中，她说，引用：“迈克尔告诉我，如果我联系警察寻求帮助或报告他的药物滥用情况，迈克尔会利用他作为医生的权力将我关进精神病院。迈克尔说他会确保我的尸体找不到。迈克尔说他会夺走我的生命。我为自己和儿子的生命感到恐惧。”

00:16:13.990 --> 00:16:29.800
<v Dana Chivvis>LaPaglia told police his girlfriend was delusional. He was arrested and charged with two drug felonies, intent to distribute a controlled substance, and intent to distribute marijuana. He pled no contest and went to inpatient rehab. He was addicted to Valium at the time.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚告诉警察他的女友有妄想症。他被逮捕并被指控犯有两项毒品重罪，意图分发受控物质和意图分发大麻。他没有抗辩，并进入了住院康复中心。他当时对安定药成瘾。

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:46.660
<v Dana Chivvis>He lost his job at the hospital. The medical board put him on probation. And he surrendered his DEA registration number, which is the thing you have to have to prescribe controlled substances. That's a big deal. It's very hard to get a job once you've lost your DEA number.
<v Dana Chivvis>他失去了在医院的工作。医疗委员会让他处于缓刑期。他上交了他的DEA注册号码，这是开具受控物质所必须的东西。这是一件大事。一旦失去DEA号码，很难找到工作。

00:16:46.660 --> 00:17:09.180
<v Rebecca Allensworth>No one wants to touch him. No employer is going to take him-- very few employers anyway-- with a record like that. Private insurance, forget it. Hospital, forget it. A few years later, he files for bankruptcy because there's no way to earn money back at the same clip as you are as an ER physician.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>没人想碰他。没有雇主会录用他——反正雇主很少——带着那样的记录。私人保险，想都别想。医院，想都别想。几年后，他申请了破产，因为不可能再像作为急诊医生那样赚回同样多的钱。

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:23.820
<v Dana Chivvis>But he got a job at an addiction clinic a friend of his owned. It was called EHC, Express Health Care. LaPaglia would see patients like a regular doctor, work up a treatment plan and everything, and then another doctor would write their prescriptions.
<v Dana Chivvis>但他从他朋友拥有的一个成瘾诊所得到了一份工作。它叫EHC，快递健康护理。拉帕利亚会像普通医生一样看病，制定治疗计划等等，然后另一位医生会开出处方。

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:41.910
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. LaPaglia helped a friend of his, Dr. Charles Brooks, get a job at Express Health Care, too. Dr. Brooks was also on probation with the medical board. He'd had sex with a patient-- he says they were having an affair-- and at least once given her a controlled substance-- he says to calm her nerves because they were having an affair.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚医生也帮助他的朋友查尔斯·布鲁克斯医生在快递健康护理中心找到了一份工作。布鲁克斯医生也处于医疗委员会的缓刑期。他曾与一名患者发生过性关系——他说他们是在搞外遇——并且至少有一次给了她一种受控物质——他说是因为他们有外遇，为了让她镇定下来。

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:54.850
<v Dana Chivvis>But unlike Dr. LaPaglia, he did not lose his DEA number. He could still prescribe. Dr. LaPaglia left EHC. And in early 2018, he and Dr. Brooks started up a little side business.
<v Dana Chivvis>但与拉帕利亚医生不同的是，他没有失去DEA号码。他仍然可以开处方。拉帕利亚医生离开了EHC。2018年初，他和布鲁克斯医生开始了一个小副业。

00:17:54.850 --> 00:18:10.170
<v Rebecca Allensworth>So he and Dr. Brooks kind of hatched this plan. LaPaglia and Brooks realized, OK, well, here's what we're going to do. We're going to start a clinic called L&B Healthcare, LaPaglia and Brooks. But LaPaglia doesn't have a DEA number, so we'll use Brooks's.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>所以他和布鲁克斯制定了这个计划。拉帕利亚和布鲁克斯意识到，好吧，我们就这么干。我们将成立一个叫L&B医疗的诊所，拉帕利亚和布鲁克斯。但拉帕利亚没有DEA号码，所以我们用布鲁克斯的。

00:18:10.170 --> 00:18:33.360
<v Rebecca Allensworth>LaPaglia is going to do the work. He was going to write the prescriptions. It's his phone number on the prescription pad that they make out. But it's Brooks's DEA number, and it's Brooks's signature. Sometimes they were presigned. Sometimes LaPaglia forged the signature. Oh, and they sell them for $300 cash out of LaPaglia's home, at least once in a McDonald's parking lot.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>拉帕利亚将去做这份工作。他要写处方。处方笺上的电话号码是他们的。但那是布鲁克斯的DEA号码，也是布鲁克斯的签名。有时它们是预先签名的。有时拉帕利亚伪造签名。哦，他们至少有一次在麦当劳的停车场，以300美元现金的价格售卖这些处方。

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:52.320
<v Dana Chivvis>L&B Healthcare didn't have an actual brick and mortar office. LaPaglia would see people wherever it was convenient, write prescriptions for controlled substances, like Klonopin, Valium, and Suboxone. When the facts came out about their operation, it looked like they were selling prescriptions for cash, like drug dealers with stethoscopes.
<v Dana Chivvis>L&B医疗并没有实际的实体办公室。拉帕利亚会在任何方便的地方看病人，为受控物质开具处方，如氯硝西泮、安定和舒必松。当关于他们的行动的事实浮出水面时，看起来他们就是在卖处方换钱，就像带着听诊器的毒贩。

00:18:52.320 --> 00:19:09.070
<v Dana Chivvis>LaPaglia and Brooks say that's not the case at all, that they were legitimately treating people with addiction problems by prescribing them controlled substances. It's something called medication-assisted treatment, like the way methadone is used to treat heroin addictions. So that's what they said they were doing.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚和布鲁克斯说根本不是那么回事，他们是在通过开具受控物质来合法地治疗有成瘾问题的患者。这叫做药物辅助治疗，就像美沙酮被用来治疗海洛因成瘾一样。所以这就是他们所说的正在做的事情。

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:36.390
<v Dana Chivvis>Regardless, it was still illegal. A few months into the venture, the Feds busted them, charged them with drug trafficking, for writing prescriptions for controlled substances without the authority to do so, and fraud for using Brooks's DEA number in the scam. Brooks pled not guilty, but Dr. LaPaglia signed a plea deal with federal prosecutors. He's still waiting to be sentenced, but he could be facing prison time.
<v Dana Chivvis>无论如何，这仍然是非法的。在冒险开始几个月后，联邦调查局抓获了他们，指控他们进行毒品交易，因为他们在没有授权的情况下开具受控物质处方，并欺诈地使用了布鲁克斯的DEA号码。布鲁克斯拒不认罪，但拉帕利亚医生与联邦检察官签署了认罪协议。他还在等待判决，但他可能面临监禁。

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:55.380
<v Dana Chivvis>So that's the federal criminal court. LaPaglia still had to face the Tennessee Medical Board. And when they found out about L&B Healthcare, they summarily suspended the doctors' medical licenses until they could hold disciplinary hearings for them. The case unfolded over three hearings from March to July 2019.
<v Dana Chivvis>这就是联邦刑事法庭的情况。拉帕利亚仍然必须面对田纳西州医疗委员会。当他们发现L&B医疗的事情时，他们立刻暂停了这两位医生的医疗执照，直到他们可以为他们举行纪律听证会。案件在2019年3月至7月的三个听证会上展开。

00:19:55.380 --> 00:20:05.530
<v Dana Chivvis>The first one in March was the disciplinary hearing for LaPaglia's partner, Dr. Brooks. Rebecca was there. She'd been going to medical board meetings for a year at that point.
<v Dana Chivvis>3月的第一次听证会是拉帕利亚的合伙人布鲁克斯医生的纪律听证会。丽贝卡就在现场。在那之前，她已经参加了一年的医疗委员会会议。

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:30.500
<v Rebecca Allensworth>And I see this guy who is doing something that actually even I think the physicians on the board find really offensive, which is giving up control of your pad. And to hand it over to somebody else is really seen as pretty unethical. Most doctors don't view their pad as a moneymaker.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>我看到这个人做的事情甚至我认为委员会里的内科医生也觉得非常冒犯，那就是放弃对自己处方权的控制。把它交给别人确实被认为是非常不道德的。大多数医生并不把他们的处方本视为赚钱工具。

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:49.450
<v Dana Chivvis>These medical board disciplinary hearings are technically administrative courts. They run kind of like the criminal and civil court systems. But the question they're addressing is more pointed. It's whether a doctor should be allowed to continue to practice medicine. Dr. LaPaglia agreed to testify against Dr. Brooks in front of the medical board.
<v Dana Chivvis>这些医疗委员会纪律听证会技术上是行政法庭。它们的运作方式有点像刑事和民事法庭系统。但他们解决的问题更尖锐。就是是否应该允许医生继续行医。拉帕利亚医生同意在医疗委员会面前作证反对布鲁克斯医生。

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:52.970
<v Andrew Coffman>The signature on that prescription says Charles Brooks, correct?
<v Andrew Coffman>那张处方上的签名是查尔斯·布鲁克斯，对吗？

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:54.220
<v Michael Lapaglia>Correct.
