00:30:20.090 --> 00:30:32.830
<v Andrew Coffman>Now I think that the evidence is going to show that Dr. LaPaglia believed that he was providing good treatment to people that actually had a need for that treatment.
<v Andrew Coffman>现在我认为证据将表明，拉帕利亚医生相信他是在为那些真正需要这种治疗的人提供良好的治疗。

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:54.480
<v Dana Chivvis>Coffman is there to prosecute LaPaglia, but it seems like he's going pretty easy on him, like that 2013 drug bust, the one where the cops found Valium, Oxycodone, amphetamines, marijuana, a gun, a scale, a ledger, paralytic agents, and other sedatives. Coffman doesn't say the charge was possession with intent to deliver. He just says LaPaglia pled to some charges--
<v Dana Chivvis>考夫曼在那里起诉拉帕利亚，但看起来他对他很宽容，比如2013年的那次毒品搜查，警察发现了安定、羟考酮、安非他命、大麻、一把枪、一个秤、一本分类账、麻痹剂和其他镇静剂。考夫曼没有说指控是持有并意图分发。他只是说拉帕利亚对一些指控进行了认罪——

00:30:54.480 --> 00:31:02.880
<v Andrew Coffman>That related to his own personal use of controlled substances, outside the bounds of the law. And the very next month--
<v Andrew Coffman>这与他个人在法律范围之外使用受控物质有关。就在下一个月——

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:06.930
<v Rebecca Allensworth>If I were prosecuting, I would probably use some stronger language.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>如果我是检察官，我可能会使用一些更强硬的语言。

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:25.530
<v Dana Chivvis>LaPaglia is coming across as a guy who once struggled with a substance use disorder, but got help, sobered up, and then, in the course of trying to help other people who suffer from drug addictions, unwittingly bumbled his way into this next bit of trouble with Dr. Brooks. Here's how LaPaglia's defense attorney also described him in his opening statement.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚给人的印象是一个曾经与物质使用障碍作斗争，但得到了帮助、变得清醒，然后在试图帮助其他遭受毒瘾折磨的人的过程中，无意中搞砸了与布鲁克斯医生的下一件麻烦事。这是拉帕利亚的辩护律师在开场白中对他描述的方式。

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:33.870
<v Attorney>He may be dumb, but he's a good doctor. He's responsible. He's done a lot to help the community.
<v Attorney>他可能很笨，但他是一位好医生。他负责任。他为社区做了很多贡献。

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:41.760
<v Dana Chivvis>It's like everyone in this trial was on Dr. LaPaglia's side.
<v Dana Chivvis>就像这场审判中的每个人都站在拉帕利亚医生一边。

00:31:41.760 --> 00:32:09.060
<v Dana Chivvis>I asked Andrew Coffman why he handled the case the way he did. He said, basically, this case wasn't contentious by its very nature. His job was to prove that Dr. LaPaglia Paglia had written prescriptions for controlled substances using another doctor's prescription pad. His star witness? Dr. LaPaglia, who had admitted repeatedly that yes, he had used Dr. Brooks's pad to write prescriptions for controlled substances.
<v Dana Chivvis>我问安德鲁·考夫曼为什么要这样处理这个案子。他说，基本上，这个案子本质上就没有争议。他的工作是证明拉帕利亚医生使用另一位医生的处方本开具了受控物质处方。他的明星证人？拉帕利亚医生本人，他反复承认是的，他用了布鲁克斯医生的本子开具受控物质处方。

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:32.900
<v Dana Chivvis>But also, there's another reason why the prosecution was maybe a bit anemic in this case. The Department of Health attorneys, Coffman and his colleagues, were feeling frustrated. When this whole thing started back in March at Dr. Brooks's hearing-- Dr. Brooks, LaPaglia's coconspirator-- Coffman had asked the board to revoke Dr. Brooks's license. But the board rejected that and said it was too harsh.
<v Dana Chivvis>但同时，起诉书在这个案子中显得有点贫血还有另一个原因。卫生部的律师们，考夫曼和他的同事们，感到很沮丧。当这一切在3月份布鲁克斯医生的听证会上开始时——布鲁克斯是拉帕利亚的同谋——考夫曼曾要求委员会吊销布鲁克斯医生的执照。但委员会拒绝了，说太严厉了。

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:50.930
<v Dana Chivvis>So the prosecutors went easier on LaPaglia in his plea deal. And then the board rejected that, said it went too easy on him. There was no consistency to their logic. Coffman's boss said later at a board retreat where they discussed this case that they felt a little bit knocked down by the whole thing.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以检察官在拉帕利亚的认罪协议中更加宽容。然后委员会拒绝了，说对他太容易了。他们的逻辑没有任何一致性。考夫曼的老板后来在一次委员会研讨会上讨论这个案子时说，他们觉得被整个事情搞得有点消沉。

00:32:50.930 --> 00:33:17.280
<v Dana Chivvis>So at LaPaglia's hearing, Coffman just presented the straight facts of the case and told the panel what their options were for disciplining him, didn't suggest a punishment. They didn't know what the hell the board wanted, so they didn't try to guess. And this brings us to the single biggest problem Rebecca sees in the system-- the doctors who sit on these medical boards.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以在拉帕利亚的听证会上，考夫曼只是陈述了案件的直白事实，并告诉委员会成员他们有什么纪律处分选择，没有建议惩罚。他们不知道委员会到底想要什么，所以他们没有试图猜测。这带来了丽贝卡在系统中看到的单一最大问题——坐在这些医疗委员会里的医生们。

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:41.450
<v Dana Chivvis>It's a distinguished position to be on the Tennessee Board of Medical Examiners. They have to be appointed by the governor. There is a full-time staff that supports the board-- attorneys, administrators, medical consultants. And they sit in judgment of doctors. But as far as judges go, they're amateurs. Most of them are doctors. They have lots of training in medicine and very little training in administrative law.
<v Dana Chivvis>进入田纳西州医疗审查委员会是一个杰出的职位。他们必须由州长任命。有一个支持委员会的全职工作人员——律师、行政人员、医疗顾问。他们对医生进行评判。但就法官而言，他们是业余爱好者。他们中的大多数是医生。他们在医学方面有大量的培训，在行政法方面的培训却很少。

00:33:41.450 --> 00:34:00.360
<v Dana Chivvis>The deciders in Dr. LaPaglia's case, the three board members on this panel, were Julianne Cole-- she's a consumer member-- Dr. Phyllis Miller, an OB-GYN, and Dr. Stephen Loyd. Dr. Loyd was the newest member of the board. He'd never done any of this before. In fact, this was his second day on the job.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚医生案件的决策者，这个小组的三名委员会成员，是朱莉安娜·科尔——她是消费者成员——菲利斯·米勒医生（妇产科医生）和斯蒂芬·劳埃德医生。劳埃德医生是委员会的新成员。他从来没有做过任何这样的事情。事实上，这是他上任的第二天。

00:34:00.360 --> 00:34:05.430
<v Stephen Loyd>I hadn't even had orientation. I mean, I walked in there-- and never been to a board meeting. I read all the rulings--
<v Stephen Loyd>我甚至还没参加迎新会。我的意思是，我走进去——从来没参加过委员会会议。我读了所有的裁决——

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:13.110
<v Dana Chivvis>When they were like, we're going to split off into panels for the contested case hearings now, did you have any sense of what that meant or what it would look like?
<v Dana Chivvis>当他们说，我们要分成小组进行有争议的案件听证会时，你是否有任何感觉这意味着什么或者它看起来会是什么样子？

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:15.090
<v Stephen Loyd>None. Nope, none.
<v Stephen Loyd>完全没有。不，完全没有。

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:20.170
<v Dana Chivvis>So what did you do? Did somebody tell you, you go into this room with these people?
<v Dana Chivvis>那么你做了什么？有没有人告诉你，你和这些人一起进那个房间？

00:34:20.170 --> 00:34:22.220
<v Stephen Loyd>[LAUGHS] What did I do?
<v Stephen Loyd>[笑] 我做了什么？

00:34:22.220 --> 00:34:22.740
<v Dana Chivvis>Yeah.
<v Dana Chivvis>是的。

00:34:22.740 --> 00:34:38.040
<v Stephen Loyd>I shit a sideways brick is what I did. I mean, I had no idea. It's just a very confusing scene the first time you're in because I just didn't understand the decorum. I didn't understand how this proceeded.
<v Stephen Loyd>我吓得要死，这就是我做的。我的意思是，我完全不知道。这是你第一次参加时非常混乱的场面，因为我不理解礼仪。我不理解这是如何进行的。

00:34:38.040 --> 00:34:53.160
<v Stephen Loyd>I mean, I'm sitting in there. And when the guy who came in who looked like a judge and talked like a judge, but what does this look like? Is this real court? Am I the jury? And if I am the jury, do I behave the same way a jury behaves?
<v Stephen Loyd>我的意思是，我坐在那里。当那个看起来像法官、说话像法官的人进来时，这看起来像什么？这是真正的法庭吗？我是陪审团吗？如果是陪审团，我的行为举止是否和陪审团一样？

00:34:53.160 --> 00:35:09.510
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. Loyd told me he did have a brief orientation that morning, but no special training, no real prep for this. Basically, he was just thrown into it. So at Dr. LaPaglia's hearing, he kind of winged it, went with his gut. And he identified with Dr. LaPaglia.
<v Dana Chivvis>劳埃德医生告诉我，他那天早上确实参加过简短的迎新，但没有特殊培训，没有任何实质性的准备。基本上，他只是被扔进去了。所以在拉帕利亚医生的听证会上，他有点临场发挥，随心所欲。他认同拉帕利亚医生。

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:18.750
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. Loyd is an addiction doctor. He spent two years as Tennessee's opioid czar. And also, like LaPaglia, he's in recovery from a pill addiction.
<v Dana Chivvis>劳埃德医生是一名成瘾医生。他曾担任田纳西州的阿片类药物沙皇两年。而且，和拉帕利亚一样，他也在从药瘾中恢复。

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:30.170
<v Stephen Loyd>And so as they're presenting this, here's this figure that I don't know the background on that looks pretty sympathetic. He's done the things you need to do. I'm a second chance guy.
<v Stephen Loyd>所以当他们展示这一切时，这里有一个我不了解背景的人物，看起来很令人同情。他做了你需要做的事情。我是个相信第二次机会的人。

00:35:30.170 --> 00:35:43.420
<v Dana Chivvis>He related to Dr. LaPaglia. He had also made bad decisions while in the throes of a drug addiction. Although to be clear, LaPaglia testified he was sober at the time he was writing the fraudulent prescriptions and had been for years.
<v Dana Chivvis>他与拉帕利亚医生有共鸣。他在毒瘾发作时也做过糟糕的决定。尽管要明确的是，拉帕利亚作证说他在开具欺诈性处方时是清醒的，并且已经清醒多年了。

00:35:43.420 --> 00:36:04.650
<v Stephen Loyd>I think I equated, hey, this guy did these things. While I didn't write scripts, I stole medication from people, which is bad. And I got better. And I think he can get better.
<v Stephen Loyd>我想我等同起来了，嘿，这个人做了这些事情。虽然我没开处方，但我从人们那里偷过药物，这很糟糕。我好转了。我想他也能好转。

00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:20.490
<v Dana Chivvis>For Loyd, LaPaglia said all the right things. He said he was drawn to treating people with addictions to benzos because that's what he was addicted to. He said he took issue with the way addiction clinics were treating patients with benzo addictions, that they weren't giving them enough pills to taper effectively.
<v Dana Chivvis>对劳埃德来说，拉帕利亚说了所有正确的话。他说他被吸引去治疗那些对苯二氮卓类药物成瘾的人，因为他自己就是对那个成瘾的。他说他不同意成瘾诊所治疗苯二氮卓类药物成瘾患者的方式，因为他们给的药丸不足以有效地戒断。

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:26.340
<v Michael Lapaglia>They were given possibly two weeks' worth of benzodiazepine and then taken off.
<v Michael Lapaglia>他们可能只给了两周的苯二氮卓类药物剂量，然后就停掉了。

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:31.060
<v Man>OK, and what would happen after they were taken off? Were they just clean of benzos?
<v Man>好的，那停掉之后会发生什么？他们是否清除了苯二氮卓类药物？

00:36:31.060 --> 00:36:32.530
<v Michael Lapaglia>It's never going to happen.
<v Michael Lapaglia>那是不可能发生的。

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:33.720
<v Man>So what would happen?
<v Man>那么会发生什么？

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:37.110
<v Michael Lapaglia>They're going to relapse 100% of the time.
<v Michael Lapaglia>他们100%会复发。

00:36:37.110 --> 00:36:50.790
<v Rebecca Allensworth>I could see Dr. Loyd nodding along. When Dr. LaPaglia was talking about the problem of dual addiction between opiates and benzos and the problem of relapse on benzos, I was like, I could see Dr. Loyd nodding along.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>我能看到劳埃德医生点头。当拉帕利亚医生谈论阿片类药物和苯二氮卓类药物的双重成瘾问题以及苯二氮卓类药物的复发问题时，我当时就在想，我能看到劳埃德医生点头。

00:36:50.790 --> 00:37:03.840
<v Stephen Loyd>What I heard from him is, I've got these patients who are addicted to benzos. These doctors don't know what they're doing, trying to get them off. And you actually can't get them off that quick. They'll just relapse. He's exactly right. That's exactly right.
<v Stephen Loyd>我从他那里听到的是，我有一些苯二氮卓类药物成瘾的病人。这些医生不知道自己在做什么，试图让他们停药。你实际上不可能那么快让他们停药。他们只会复发。他说得完全正确。完全正确。

00:37:03.840 --> 00:37:08.810
<v Dana Chivvis>After the attorneys were done questioning Dr. LaPaglia, the board members got a turn.
<v Dana Chivvis>在律师完成对拉帕利亚医生的质询后，委员会成员轮流提问。

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:12.840
<v Stephen Loyd>But first of all, thanks, Dr. LaPaglia, for coming today and being honest.
