00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:52.330
<v Michael Lapaglia>I respectfully ask no because I may change my mind, or something else may happen. And if I don't fulfill that requirement, I'm never going to get off probation.
<v Michael Lapaglia>我恭敬地请求不要，因为我可能会改变主意，或者发生别的事情。如果我没有完成那个要求，我就永远别想结束缓刑期。

00:39:52.330 --> 00:40:08.760
<v Dana Chivvis>The administrative judge has to break in and tell them to quit talking to each other. In the end, here's what the panel decides. They're going to restore Dr. LaPaglia's medical license, which had been summarily suspended in January, when the board first found out about the L&B scheme.
<v Dana Chivvis>行政法法官不得不插嘴告诉他们停止互相交谈。最终，小组委员会决定了如下内容。他们将恢复拉帕利亚医生的医疗执照，该执照在1月份委员会首次发现L&B计划时被简要暂停。

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:37.230
<v Dana Chivvis>As punishment, they give him five years probation, make him report to the Tennessee Medical Foundation. It's a physician's substance abuse group. And they tell him to take a few professional courses. They don't prohibit him from prescribing controlled substances. He still doesn't have a DEA number, so technically, he can't anyway. But he could petition the DEA to get it back. They don't restrict that either.
<v Dana Chivvis>作为惩罚，他们给他五年缓刑，让他向田纳西医学基金会汇报。那是一个医生药物滥用小组。他们告诉他去参加一些专业课程。他们没有禁止他开具受控物质。他仍然没有DEA号码，所以从技术上讲，无论如何他都开不了。但他可以向DEA申请恢复。他们也没有限制这一点。

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:44.010
<v Dana Chivvis>It's actually a lighter discipline than what he would have gotten from the plea agreement, the one the board threw out for being too easy on him.
<v Dana Chivvis>这实际上比他从认罪协议中获得的纪律处分还要轻，也就是委员会因太轻而否决的那个协议。

00:40:44.010 --> 00:41:02.120
<v Rebecca Allensworth>So yeah, it was a slap on the wrist. Dr. LaPaglia was all smiles afterwards. So I had chatted with him at the breaks. And he was sort of saying, I'm just hoping to get my license back. And I got this federal thing I got to deal with.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>所以是的，这只是轻描淡写。拉帕利亚医生事后笑逐颜开。所以我在休息时和他聊过。他当时大概是说，我只是希望能拿回执照。我还有这个联邦问题要处理。

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:15.220
<v Rebecca Allensworth>And so, for some reason, they decided to insert this language at the end that says, to give him a path back to full licensure. And when they read that out loud, Dr. LaPaglia winked at me.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>所以出于某种原因，他们决定在最后插入这段话说，给他一条通往完全执照的道路。当他们大声读出来时，拉帕利亚医生对我眨了眨眼。

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:15.980
<v Dana Chivvis>Whoa.
<v Dana Chivvis>哇。

00:41:15.980 --> 00:41:21.640
<v Rebecca Allensworth>Which really surprised me. It just really surprised me. I was like, did that just happen?
<v Rebecca Allensworth>这真的让我很惊讶。我当时就像，那是真的发生了吗？

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:23.830
<v Dana Chivvis>How did you interpret his wink?
<v Dana Chivvis>你怎么解读他的眨眼？

00:41:23.830 --> 00:41:36.200
<v Rebecca Allensworth>I think that he was sort of saying, see, I'm getting what I wanted-- I guess. I think it went even better than he expected. And I think that might be why he winked at me.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>我想他大概是说，看，我得到了我想要的——我想是这样。我想这比他预期的还要好。我想这就是他对我眨眼的原因。

00:41:36.200 --> 00:41:54.250
<v Rebecca Allensworth>So I went up to him afterwards to talk to him. And by this time, I had chatted with him a couple times. And he said something like, I'm just glad to have my license off of suspension so I can make some money. It was a real kind of 180 from the patient care testimony that he had just provided.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>所以事后我走过去和他谈话。到那时，我已经和他聊过几次了。他说了一些类似，我很高兴我的执照取消暂停，这样我可以赚点钱。这和他刚才提供的病人护理证词完全是180度大转弯。

00:41:54.250 --> 00:42:06.130
<v Dana Chivvis>I reached out to Dr. LaPaglia about this, but he didn't get back to me.
<v Dana Chivvis>我就此联系了拉帕利亚医生，但他没有回复我。

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:24.940
<v Dana Chivvis>So here you've got a doctor who lost his DEA number when he was caught with illegal prescription drugs and marijuana at his house in what looked very much like a drug trafficking operation, whose medical license was put on probation, who then went out and broke the law again by using another doctor's DEA number to prescribe controlled substances.
<v Dana Chivvis>所以在这里，你有一个医生，当他在家里被发现持有非法处方药和大麻时失去了DEA号码，这看起来非常像毒品交易，他的医疗执照被处于缓刑期，然后他又再次触犯法律，使用另一位医生的DEA号码开具受控物质。

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:49.840
<v Dana Chivvis>And now here he is, back on probation with the medical board, still with a medical license, which means he can still work as a doctor. In the years that she's been going to medical board meetings, Rebecca has watched a bunch of doctors with egregious histories keep practicing medicine, doctors who have committed health care fraud, doctors who prescribed controlled substances for people who aren't their patients, doctors who have sex with patients.
<v Dana Chivvis>而现在他在这里，再次处于医疗委员会的缓刑期，仍然持有医疗执照，这意味着他仍然可以作为医生工作。在丽贝卡参加医疗委员会会议的几年里，她看着许多有恶劣历史的医生继续行医，那些犯了医保欺诈的医生，那些为非患者的人开具受控物质的医生，那些与病人发生关系的医生。

00:42:49.840 --> 00:43:02.470
<v Dana Chivvis>One doctor prescribed large doses of opioids and other drugs, even though the patients had no medical reason for taking them. Prosecutors asked the board to permanently revoke his license, but he was only put on probation.
<v Dana Chivvis>一位医生开了大剂量的阿片类药物和其他药物，尽管病人根本没有服用它们的医学理由。检察官要求委员会永久吊销他的执照，但他只被处于缓刑期。

00:43:02.470 --> 00:43:17.070
<v Dana Chivvis>As far as I can tell, Tennessee is fairly typical. A statistical study ranked medical boards on a scale of how harsh to light their discipline was. Tennessee was smack in the middle. Of course, I'm talking about the worst cases the medical board sees.
<v Dana Chivvis>据我所知，田纳西州相当典型。一项统计研究将医疗委员会的纪律从严厉到轻微进行了排名。田纳西州处于正中间。当然，我指的是医疗委员会看到的那些最糟糕的案例。

00:43:17.070 --> 00:43:37.730
<v Dana Chivvis>And the board does revoke some doctor's licenses. Since 2017, they've taken 13 licenses away, and 11 doctors have voluntarily surrendered them. That's a significant punishment. As one board member explained to me, a medical license is technically a property right. And it's a big deal to take someone's property from them.
<v Dana Chivvis>委员会确实吊销了一些医生的执照。自2017年以来，他们收回了13个执照，11名医生自愿交出了执照。那是重大的惩罚。正如一位委员会成员向我解释的那样，医疗执照在技术上是一种财产权。拿走某人的财产是一件大事。

00:43:37.730 --> 00:43:54.730
<v Dana Chivvis>But something does seem to be happening, where medical boards, which are set up to protect the public, to protect the majority of us who never think to ask whether our doctors are safe and operating in our best interests, sometimes the medical boards are letting dangerous doctors continue to practice medicine.
<v Dana Chivvis>但似乎确实发生了什么事，本应保护公众的医疗委员会，为了保护我们这些从不想去问医生是否安全、是否符合我们最大利益的大多数人，有时医疗委员会却让危险的医生继续行医。

00:43:54.730 --> 00:44:12.710
<v Rebecca Allensworth>On the one hand, I feel like on some level, I understand this problem because I've spent so long studying it. I've seen all the angles. I've seen how the personalities play out and the incentives and the emotions and everything. But there's also a way in which I just don't get it. I don't understand why they won't just take the guy's license.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>一方面，我觉得某种程度上我理解这个问题，因为我花了这么长时间研究它。我看到了所有的角度。我看到了个性是如何展现出来的，激励、情感等等。但还有一种方式是我只是不明白。我不明白为什么他们就是不吊销那家伙的执照。

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:17.650
<v Dana Chivvis>What's the part of you that understands it?
<v Dana Chivvis>理解它的那部分你是怎么想的？

00:44:17.650 --> 00:44:39.020
<v Rebecca Allensworth>[SIGHS] I think that there's a identification that's not as specific as, oh, that could be me. That's still in play of just being a doctor. This is our calling. We went to similar sets of trainings. We started out walking down the same path.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>[叹气] 我认为有一种不那么具体的认同，比如，哦，那可能是我。那仍然是一种作为医生的认同。这是我们的使命。我们经历了类似的培训。我们最初走的是同一条路。

00:44:39.020 --> 00:45:08.950
<v Rebecca Allensworth>So I think there is some identification at a kind of very high professional level, like a circling the wagons. Like, we're doctors. And then I think there's also-- and this is probably especially bad for doctors, but they're just kind of softies. They just believe that everybody can be healed and can be made better and are sick and deserving of compassion.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>所以我认为在高专业水平上有一种认同，就像围成一圈。就像，我们是医生。然后我认为还有——这对医生来说可能特别糟糕，但他们只是有点软心肠。他们只是相信每个人都可以被治愈，可以变得更好，而且是生病的，值得同情。

00:45:08.950 --> 00:45:26.590
<v Rebecca Allensworth>I mean, that's what's really ironic and sad to me, is that it's, in some ways, this whole problem is created by that better part of human nature, the part that's full of mercy.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>我的意思是，这对我来说才是真正讽刺和可悲的地方，在某些方面，整个问题是由人性的那一更好部分创造的，那部分充满了怜悯。

00:45:26.590 --> 00:45:29.420
<v Dana Chivvis>Rebecca's solution to this whole thing?
<v Dana Chivvis>丽贝卡的解决方案是什么？

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:34.120
<v Rebecca Allensworth>Well, I think we need to abolish licensing boards, to be perfectly honest.
<v Rebecca Allensworth>嗯，说实话，我认为我们需要废除许可委员会。

00:45:34.120 --> 00:46:04.770
<v Dana Chivvis>Instead of having doctors sit in judgment of other doctors and instead of having them do this as a favor to society every few months, Rebecca says have full-time employees whose job it is to regulate the industry, who are trained to notice when they're being affected by the emotional sway of a defendant, and to put their feelings aside as much as possible, like a judge would. Dr. Loyd agrees with Rebecca, in part. He admits he did relate to LaPaglia, and it influenced how he thought about discipline.
<v Dana Chivvis>与其让医生评判其他医生，与其让他们每隔几个月做一次顺水人情，丽贝卡说，要有全职雇员，他们的工作就是监管行业，他们受过训练，能注意到自己是否受到被告情感影响，并尽可能地把自己的感受放在一边，就像法官那样。劳埃德医生部分同意丽贝卡的观点。他承认他确实对拉帕利亚产生了共鸣，这影响了他对纪律处分的看法。

00:46:04.770 --> 00:46:19.030
<v Stephen Loyd>If I saw the facts of the case written down on a piece of paper, right? Just boom, right down-- start at the top, go to the bottom. And I get to read that piece of paper without anybody from either side, right? And I just read that, I'd revoke his license.
<v Stephen Loyd>如果我把案件的事实写在纸上，对吧？就那样——从头开始，一直写到底。我读那张纸时没有来自任何一方的人，对吧？我只是读了它，我会吊销他的执照。

00:46:19.030 --> 00:46:26.230
<v Dana Chivvis>Just based on the, he used another doctor's DEA license number to prescribe patients.
<v Dana Chivvis>仅仅基于他用了另一位医生的DEA执照号码来给病人开药。

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:45.400
<v Stephen Loyd>Just based on flat facts-- drug charges, used another doctor's DEA license, drug dealing, right? Just the plain facts, no defense attorney, no prosecutor. I just saw that written down on a piece of paper, no LaPaglia in the room-- I'd revoke his license.
<v Stephen Loyd>仅基于客观事实——毒品指控，使用了另一位医生的DEA执照，毒品交易，对吧？仅仅是平铺直叙的事实，没有辩护律师，没有检察官。我只是把它写在纸上，没有拉帕利亚在房间里——我会吊销他的执照。

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:49.130
<v Dana Chivvis>Hm, that's so interesting.
<v Dana Chivvis>嗯，那真有趣。

00:46:49.130 --> 00:47:13.850
<v Stephen Loyd>And this is something-- well, it's something I've gained an appreciation for. And I'm really trying to work on this as a board member. It is very difficult to sit there with another human being in front of you and-- what I want to say-- drop the hammer.
<v Stephen Loyd>这是我获得的一种认知。作为委员会成员，我真的在努力解决这个问题。坐在那里，面前有一个人，然后——我想说的是——重锤落下，真的很困难。

00:47:13.850 --> 00:47:35.640
<v Stephen Loyd>It's a human being, right? It's much easier on a sheet of paper, right? Because when I'm sitting there, I see a guy that's presenting this way. He doesn't look emaciated. He looks healthy. He's dressed professionally. He's got a child that he has emotion around, like me as a dad. Do I want to give him a chance to get that back? Yeah, I do.
<v Stephen Loyd>这是一个人，对吧？在纸上容易得多，对吧？因为当我就坐在那里时，我看到一个表现成这样的人。他看起来并不憔悴。他看起来很健康。他穿得很专业。他有一个他有情感的孩子，就像我作为一个父亲一样。我想给他一个机会让他拿回那个吗？是的，我想。

00:47:35.640 --> 00:47:58.300
<v Dana Chivvis>But it's not fair to say that's the whole reason the case went askew, that doctors looked too kindly on a fellow doctor sitting before them. Because when considering what to do with LaPaglia's medical license and whether he should be a practicing doctor, the board members never got a full airing of his past misdeeds. As soon as the hearing was over, Dr. LaPaglia did something that made Dr. Loyd suspicious.
<v Dana Chivvis>但说那是案件出错的全部原因并不公平，医生对坐在他们面前的同行太宽容了。因为在考虑如何处理拉帕利亚的医疗执照以及他是否应该成为一名行医的医生时，委员会成员从来没有全面地了解他过去的恶行。听证会一结束，拉帕利亚医生就做了一件让劳埃德医生怀疑的事情。

00:47:58.300 --> 00:48:21.820
<v Stephen Loyd>I stood up and was headed out, and he approached me. And I can't remember his exact words, but it was very placating, almost like, hey, I appreciate what you've done in your own life. I would like to have that, right? That kind of thing. And I knew it was bullshit.
<v Stephen Loyd>我站起身准备离开，他走向了我。我不记得他的具体词汇了，但非常安抚人心，就像，嘿，我赞赏你在自己生活中所做的一切。我希望能有那个，对吧？那种事情。而且我知道那是狗屁。

00:48:21.820 --> 00:48:27.010
<v Dana Chivvis>He felt like he was being manipulated. Something about LaPaglia's affect felt fake.
<v Dana Chivvis>他觉得他被操纵了。拉帕利亚的某种影响感觉很假。

00:48:27.010 --> 00:48:39.310
<v Stephen Loyd>I knew I had made a mistake. My little man inside of me that I use as my barometer of people was like, oh, man, Steve, this guy is a shyster. He kissed my ass, and I couldn't fucking stand-- I'm sorry to cuss, Dana. I'm--
<v Stephen Loyd>我知道我犯了错误。我内心深处用来衡量人的晴雨表说，噢，伙计，史蒂夫，这家伙是个骗子。他拍我马屁，我他妈的受不了——抱歉，达纳。我——

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:40.330
<v Dana Chivvis>No, it's fine.
<v Dana Chivvis>不，没关系。

00:48:40.330 --> 00:49:04.450
<v Stephen Loyd>I couldn't stand it. I couldn't stand it. It wore me. So I went outside and googled him. I said goodbye to everybody. I walked out to my car. I grabbed my phone-- or my truck. I sat there, and I googled his name. And the first article that popped up in the way I searched it was the article about his domestic violence.
<v Stephen Loyd>我受不了。我受不了。它折磨着我。所以我走到外面用谷歌搜了他。我和大家道别。我走到我的车前。我抓起我的手机——或者我的卡车。我坐在那里，用谷歌搜了他的名字。我搜到的第一篇跳出来的文章就是关于他家庭暴力的文章。

00:49:04.450 --> 00:49:18.490
<v Dana Chivvis>Andrew Coffman didn't talk about the restraining order against LaPaglia at the trial. It's not a criminal charge. And besides, he said, while it clearly demonstrates bad conduct, it isn't bad conduct related to the practice of medicine.
<v Dana Chivvis>考夫曼在审判时没有谈到针对拉帕利亚的限制令。那不是刑事指控。而且此外，他说，虽然这清楚地证明了糟糕的行为，但这不是与行医相关的糟糕行为。

00:49:18.490 --> 00:49:38.140
<v Dana Chivvis>But that's not all he left out. In 2010, while working as an ER doctor, LaPaglia did something that a federal appeals court said, quote, "shocks the conscience." The police brought a young man named Felix Booker to the ER. They suspected Booker was hiding drugs on his body-- up his butt, to be blunt.
<v Dana Chivvis>但这不是他漏掉的全部。2010年，在作为急诊医生工作时，拉帕利亚做了一件事，联邦上诉法院称其“震撼了良知”。警察把一个叫菲利克斯·布克的年轻人带到了急诊室。他们怀疑布克把毒品藏在身体里——直白地说，藏在屁股里。

00:49:38.140 --> 00:49:54.340
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. LaPaglia asked Booker to consent to a rectal exam. Booker was naked, except for a blanket, and in handcuffs and leg shackles. He said no. LaPaglia says Booker consented to the exam, but nobody else who was in the room heard this. And there's no medical record of it.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚医生要求布克同意进行直肠检查。布克全身赤裸，只有一条毯子，还戴着手铐和脚镣。他说不。拉帕利亚说布克同意了检查，但房间里的其他人没有人听到这个。而且没有关于此的医疗记录。

00:49:54.340 --> 00:50:17.550
<v Dana Chivvis>LaPaglia tried to examine him. Booker clenched his muscles, so LaPaglia had him sedated. Booker was still able to clench his muscles down, so LaPaglia had him medically paralyzed, intubated him, and did the rectal exam. He found a rock of crack. LaPaglia testified at Booker's trial that he'd done rectal exams on two other people the police had brought in to him.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚试图检查他。布克咬紧肌肉，所以拉帕利亚让他服用了镇静剂。布克仍然能够咬紧肌肉，所以拉帕利亚让他医疗性麻痹，插管并进行了直肠检查。他发现了一块快克毒品。拉帕利亚在布克的审判中作证说，他曾对警察带给他的另外两个人做过直肠检查。

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:40.650
<v Dana Chivvis>A year after that, there was a fourth guy, Wesley Gulley. Gulley alleged he got the same treatment. LaPaglia asked him to consent to a rectal exam. He said no. LaPaglia threatened to sedate him and paralyze him, so he relented. LaPaglia did the rectal exam and found nothing. The police charged Gulley with resisting arrest. The hospital charged him for the rectal exam.
<v Dana Chivvis>那之后的一年，有第四个人，卫斯理·格利。格利声称他遭到了同样的待遇。拉帕利亚要求他同意直肠检查。他说不。拉帕利亚威胁要让他服用镇静剂并让他瘫痪，所以他屈服了。拉帕利亚做了直肠检查，什么也没发现。警察指控格利拒捕。医院对他进行了直肠检查收费。

00:50:40.650 --> 00:50:56.040
<v Dana Chivvis>But apparently, nothing ever happened to Dr. LaPaglia. He wasn't criminally charged for the warrantless, no-consent rectal searches. He didn't even lose his job at the hospital. The searches were reported to the Department of Health, but Dr. LaPaglia was not disciplined for them.
<v Dana Chivvis>但显然，拉帕利亚医生从未受到任何惩罚。他没有因为非法、未经同意的直肠搜索而被刑事指控。他甚至没有失去在医院的工作。搜索事件被报告给了卫生部，但拉帕利亚医生没有因此受到纪律处分。

00:50:56.040 --> 00:51:11.250
<v Dana Chivvis>And in a cruel regulatory irony, because he was not disciplined, there's no public record of why the board did not discipline him. Dr. Loyd actually found out about the rectal searches from Rebecca. They had coffee a few weeks after Dr. LaPaglia's hearing.
<v Dana Chivvis>在一个残酷的监管讽刺中，因为他没有受到纪律处分，没有关于为什么委员会没有对他进行纪律处分的公开记录。劳埃德医生实际上是从丽贝卡那里得知直肠检查的事。在拉帕利亚医生听证会几周后，他们喝了咖啡。

00:51:11.250 --> 00:51:22.960
<v Stephen Loyd>I mean, that's bringing up emotions right now I can't even-- that's somebody who's taken advantage of their position of power. That's not forgivable. Maybe God could forgive him, but I can't.
<v Stephen Loyd>我的意思是，那现在激起了我甚至无法——那是有人利用了他们的权力地位。这是不可原谅的。也许上帝能宽恕他，但我不能。

00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:44.070
<v Stephen Loyd>I can tell you this. If he paralyzed my son in the ER, I would have fucking killed him. I mean, I would've. I would've went after him. I don't care. They just said, you know, well, you're going to-- you know, you want to pay consequences for this, Steve? Fine. He paralyzed him. He paralyzed his muscles against his consent. The guy did not give consent for that.
<v Stephen Loyd>我可以告诉你这个。如果他在急诊室让我儿子瘫痪，我他妈会杀了他。我的意思是，我会的。我会追杀他。我不在乎。他们只是说，你知道，好吧，你要——你知道，你想为这个付出代价吗，史蒂夫？好。他让他瘫痪了。他未经同意让他肌肉瘫痪了。那个人根本没有给那个同意。

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:49.440
<v Dana Chivvis>Yeah, he had to be intubated. I mean, he couldn't even breathe on his own.
<v Dana Chivvis>是的，他必须插管。我的意思是，他甚至无法自己呼吸。

00:51:49.440 --> 00:52:04.110
<v Stephen Loyd>No, no, you can't. I mean, you paralyze all muscles, and your diaphragm is a muscle. So you're not going to able to breathe. Yeah, and that's somebody who will do anything. That's just incomprehensible to me.
<v Stephen Loyd>不，不，你不能。我的意思是，你让所有肌肉瘫痪，你的横膈膜就是肌肉。所以你不可能能够呼吸。是的，那是会做任何事的人。这对我来说简直是不可理解的。

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:27.540
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. Loyd and the other board members on LaPaglia's panel, all they knew was that LaPaglia had been on probation with the board once before for charges related to his personal drug use, that he'd been treated for a pill addiction and had stayed sober all these years, and that he and Dr. Brooks got caught selling prescriptions using Dr. Brooks's DEA number, which is a way tidier story than the one I just told you.
<v Dana Chivvis>劳埃德医生和拉帕利亚小组的其他委员会成员，他们所知道的只是拉帕利亚因为与个人吸毒有关的指控曾一次受到委员会的缓刑，他因为药瘾接受了治疗，多年来一直保持清醒，他和布鲁克斯医生因为使用布鲁克斯的DEA号码销售处方而被抓，这是一个比我刚才告诉你的故事整洁得多的故事。

00:52:27.540 --> 00:52:49.150
<v Dana Chivvis>But Coffman didn't talk about the rectal searches at Dr. LaPaglia's hearing because of the rules of evidence, he told me. It wouldn't have been admissible. Same for the details about the 2014 drug charges, which, by the way, after LaPaglia pled no contest, he was put into something called judicial diversion. It's a kind of probation. And eventually, the case was dismissed.
<v Dana Chivvis>但考夫曼在拉帕利亚医生的听证会上没有谈论直肠检查，因为证据规则，他告诉我。那本来是不可采纳的。关于2014年毒品指控的细节也是如此，顺便说一句，在拉帕利亚没有抗辩后，他被置于一种叫司法分流的系统中。那是一种缓刑。最终，案件被撤销了。

00:52:49.150 --> 00:53:01.590
<v Dana Chivvis>But more importantly, Coffman said this wasn't a hearing about everything LaPaglia had ever done-- just this one crime, the fraudulent prescriptions. Dr. Loyd didn't learn about the rest of it until it was too late.
<v Dana Chivvis>但更重要的是，考夫曼说这不是关于拉帕利亚曾经做过的所有事情的听证会——只是这一个罪行，欺诈性处方。劳埃德医生直到为时已晚才了解到其余的事情。

00:53:01.590 --> 00:53:16.840
<v Stephen Loyd>When I'm looking at him up there, I mean, it looked like he was doing what he was supposed to be doing. He's trying his best. Sure, that's what I felt. And I think I am a good judge of that, given all the facts. But I didn't have all the facts.
<v Stephen Loyd>当我在那里看着他时，我的意思是，看起来他正在做他应该做的事情。他在尽力。肯定，那就是我的感觉。而且我认为我是个很好的判断者，考虑到所有的事实。但我没有所有的事实。

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:30.500
<v Stephen Loyd>How many strikes do I believe that somebody gets, Dana? As many as they need. In the real world, there's no such thing as the third strike to me. But this is not the real world. You have a professional medical license, and you can harm others. There is a strike limit there.
<v Stephen Loyd>我认为某人有几次机会，达纳？只要他们需要多少次就有多少次。在现实世界中，对我来说没有第三次机会这回事。但这不是现实世界。你拥有专业医疗执照，你可以伤害他人。那里有一个罢工限制。

00:53:30.500 --> 00:53:44.990
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. Loyd says he asks a lot more questions now during these cases. So how to keep this from happening? How to make sure the board gets a full picture of a doctor's career and past misdeeds before they decide how to discipline them?
<v Dana Chivvis>劳埃德医生说他现在在这些案件中问的问题多了很多。那么如何防止这种情况发生？如何确保委员会在决定如何惩罚医生之前得到医生职业生涯和过去恶行的全面画面？

00:53:44.990 --> 00:54:04.960
<v Dana Chivvis>Rebecca points to a simple and practical solution-- separate the disciplinary hearing into two parts. Have one hearing where the board hears evidence and decides if a doctor is guilty of the charges, and another hearing where the doctor is disciplined. And at that point, they can learn other information about a defendant's past, both good and bad.
<v Dana Chivvis>丽贝卡提出了一个简单而实用的解决方案——将纪律听证会分为两部分。举行一次听证会，委员会听取证据并决定医生是否有罪，另一次听证会医生受到惩罚。在那一点上，他们可以了解被告过去的、无论好的还是坏的其他信息。

00:54:04.960 --> 00:54:28.260
<v Dana Chivvis>That's how criminal courts work. Once the jury has determined a person's guilt, the judge is the one who does the sentencing and gets to hear more about the defendant. This solution, Rebecca points out, has the benefit of being readily available to the board. It's already legal under Tennessee law.
<v Dana Chivvis>刑事法庭就是那样运作的。一旦陪审团确定了一个人的罪行，法官就是那个进行量刑的人，并有机会听到更多关于被告的事情。丽贝卡指出，这个解决方案的好处是委员会可以立即使用。根据田纳西州法律，这已经是合法的了。

00:54:28.260 --> 00:54:44.280
<v Dana Chivvis>Last September, the medical board members and staff had an offsite retreat. Rebecca was there. She recorded it for her notes for her book on licensing boards. And one of the things they discussed was Dr. LaPaglia's case, what had gone wrong. Dr. Loyd was still upset about it.
<v Dana Chivvis>去年9月，医疗委员会成员和工作人员举行了外部研讨会。丽贝卡在现场。她把录音记在了她关于许可委员会的书的笔记里。他们讨论的事情之一是拉帕利亚医生的案件，哪里出了问题。劳埃德医生对此仍然感到不安。

00:54:44.280 --> 00:55:04.000
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. LaPaglia is a practicing doctor in Tennessee right now. He does house calls for $50 cash. And Loyd explained what he sees as the real problem with that kind of outcome, with this halfway discipline they gave LaPaglia. They've made it so he can't get a job at a hospital or a medical clinic, but he can still see patients.
<v Dana Chivvis>拉帕利亚医生现在是田纳西州的一名执业医生。他以50美元现金进行家庭出诊。劳埃德解释了他所认为的那种结果的真正问题，这种他们给拉帕利亚的半吊子纪律处分。他们搞得他无法在医院或诊所找到工作，但他仍然可以看病人。

00:55:04.000 --> 00:55:30.580
<v Dana Chivvis>Dr. Loyd said, quote, "He can only work in a cash-based practice, and so the most vulnerable Tennesseans are the ones at risk. And that's not awesome because the very population that I work personally with on a daily basis. I just put somebody back out there, and I'm struggling with that."
<v Dana Chivvis>劳埃德医生说，引用：“他只能在现金为主的诊所工作，所以最脆弱的田纳西人是面临风险的人。这并不好，因为那正是每天我个人工作的人群。我刚刚把某人重新放出去，我正在为此挣扎。”

00:55:30.580 --> 00:55:38.980
<v Ira Glass>Dana Chivvis is one of the producers of our program. Rebecca Allensworth wrote about medical boards and Dr. LaPaglia's case for the New York Review of Books.
<v Ira Glass>达纳·奇维斯是我们节目的制片人之一。丽贝卡·艾伦斯沃思为《纽约书评》撰写了关于医疗委员会和拉帕利亚案件的文章。

